# 0024. `/log`: the whole thread as Markdown in the configured editor

- Status: Accepted (owner, 2026-11-25)
- Date: 2026-11-25
- Replaces: [0013](0013-log-command.adr) (rejected)

Copying several lines out of the TUI picks up pane borders and wrap breaks, because
terminal copy works on screen cells. `/log` copies the whole current thread as Markdown
into a temp file and opens it in the editor set in the config (nvim in the default
config); when the editor session closes, the file is deleted.

## Technical details
- **Command**: `/log`, no arguments: always the whole current thread. Added to the command
  table ([0008](0008-slash-commands-and-mentions.adr), `commands::COMMANDS` +
  `App::command`). No thread yet: "nothing to log yet", no file.
- **Editor from the config, not from code**: a top-level key in `models.toml`
  ([0011](0011-hot-model-registry.adr)), hot like the rest of the file:
  ```toml
  # The editor /log opens the thread in; the file is deleted when it closes.
  editor = "nvim"
  ```
  - The default file roundtable writes on first start contains `editor = "nvim"`. The
    code has no built-in editor name and doesn't read `$EDITOR`/`$VISUAL`: the config is
    the one place it is set.
  - The value is a command line, split with shell-words, so `editor = "nvim -R"` or
    `editor = "code --wait"` work; the file path is added as the last argument.
  - Key missing or empty: `/log` answers "set `editor` in models.toml" and writes no
    file. Validation is the registry's usual all-or-nothing parse (a non-string `editor`
    is an error with its line).
- **The file**: the whole thread rendered as Markdown from its transcript
  (`events.jsonl`, [0010](0010-filesystem-event-log.adr)), never from the screen, so
  there are no borders or wrap breaks, and key values are already redacted:
  ```
  # <thread title> · 2026-11-25

  ## you · 19:41
  @codex review this

  ## codex · 19:42
  <reply, verbatim>

  > claude → codex (ask)
  > <prompt>
  >
  > <answer>
  ```
  Messages verbatim (code blocks keep their fences), `ask` exchanges in full as quotes,
  tool calls one line each, errors as `> ! <text>`. A pure function
  `log::render(&[EventLine]) -> String` in the library, covered by insta snapshots.
- **Temp file**: in the system temp folder (`std::env::temp_dir()`, the per-user
  `$TMPDIR` on macOS), named `roundtable-<ulid>.md`, created with `create_new` and mode
  0600. Deleted as soon as the editor process exits, whatever its exit code. A
  `roundtable-*.md` left behind by a crash is removed at the next start.
- **Opening**: roundtable leaves the screen and runs the editor in the same terminal: the
  terminal-input task pauses (else it steals the editor's keys), raw mode and the alternate
  screen are turned off, the editor runs and is waited for, then the screen is restored
  and fully redrawn. Replies keep streaming into `App` meanwhile; only drawing pauses.
  The editor not found, or exiting non-zero: an error line in the chat, the file deleted.
- **State/IO split** ([0002](0002-single-owner-event-loop.adr)): `App::update` returns
  `Effect::OpenLog`; the loop reads the transcript, renders, writes the file and runs the
  editor.
- **A snapshot**: the file holds the thread as it was at `/log`; edits to it are
  discarded with the file.
- Consequences: copying, searching and reading back use the owner's own editor and keys,
  with no in-TUI selection. Costs: a Markdown render to maintain next to the TUI's, and the
  terminal hand-over to the editor.

## Technologies and alternatives
- Involved: crossterm (raw mode, alternate screen), `shell-words`, ulid, insta
- Alternatives:
  - `$VISUAL`/`$EDITOR` with nvim as fallback (0013): the owner wants it in the config.
  - `/log <n>` for the last messages (0013): the agreement is the whole thread.
  - A tmux popup instead of leaving the screen (0013): a second launch path; can come
    later as an option of this one.
  - In-TUI selection with mouse capture: breaks tmux mouse selection over the pane.
